# Template for `.dev.vars`, which wrangler reads when you run `npm run preview`.
# Copy this file to `.dev.vars` and fill it in. `.dev.vars` is gitignored;
# THIS file is committed, so never put a real key in it.
#
# The same names go into the Cloudflare dashboard for the deployed site:
#   Workers & Pages -> the project -> Settings -> Variables and Secrets
#   Add them to BOTH the Production and Preview environments.
#   RESEND_API_KEY and TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY must be of type "Secret".
#
# ONE NAME DOES NOT BELONG HERE: NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY. See the
# Turnstile section at the bottom — it is a build variable, not a runtime one,
# and putting it in this file has no effect whatsoever.

# Resend -> API Keys -> Create. Sending permission is enough.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Where leads are delivered.
# While you are on Resend's shared `onboarding@resend.dev` sender, this MUST be
# the address the Resend account was created with — the sandbox refuses every
# other recipient. Change it to the firm's inbox once a domain is verified.
CONTACT_TO=you@example.com

# Who the mail is from. `onboarding@resend.dev` works with no DNS setup at all.
# Becomes e.g. "WVP Plus Consulting <info@wvpconsulting.com>" after verification.
CONTACT_FROM=onboarding@resend.dev

# Set to 1 to log the message to the wrangler console instead of sending it.
# Lets you test the whole form before you have a Resend account. Never set in
# production — it makes the form silently discard leads again.
CONTACT_DRY_RUN=1


# --- Turnstile (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The form works without any of this. Leaving TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY unset skips
# the check completely; only the honeypot runs. Turning it on takes TWO keys
# from one dashboard widget (Cloudflare -> Turnstile -> Add widget), and they
# are read at two different times, which is the whole trap:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY   public, read when the site is BUILT
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY             secret, read when a form is SUBMITTED
#
# Because the site key is compiled into the JavaScript, adding it in the
# dashboard changes nothing until the next deploy.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Add the site key and redeploy FIRST, confirm the widget
# appears on /mk/kontakt, and only then add the secret. The other way round
# gives you a deployed site whose pages cannot produce a token and a server
# that demands one — every genuine enquiry refused.
#
# To turn it OFF again, delete the secret. The check stops immediately, with no
# redeploy needed.
#
# Add the widget's hostnames in the dashboard: the custom domain AND
# <project>.pages.dev, or the previews will fail the challenge.
#
# LOCALLY, the two keys go in two different files:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY -> .env.local   (read by `next build`)
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY           -> .dev.vars    (read by wrangler)
# Both are gitignored. Cloudflare's public test keys below always pass, so you
# can exercise the whole path without creating a widget at all:
#
#   .env.local:  NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
#   .dev.vars:   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#
# Swap in 2x00000000000000000000AB / 2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA to
# test the refusal path instead.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=


# --- Admin panel (/admin) ----------------------------------------------------
#
# All six are required; until they are set, /admin says it is not set up.
# In the dashboard, make every one except GITHUB_REPO and GITHUB_BRANCH a Secret.
#
# The sign-in. Run `node scripts/admin-credentials.mjs` and paste its two lines.
ADMIN_USERNAME=
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
#
# Saving a post commits to this repository, and Cloudflare rebuilds from it.
# GitHub -> Settings -> Developer settings -> Fine-grained tokens -> Generate:
#   Repository access: only this repository
#   Permissions: Contents -> Read and write (nothing else)
# Locally, point GITHUB_BRANCH at a throwaway branch, never the production one:
# every save is a real commit.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GITHUB_REPO=owner/wvp-consulting
GITHUB_BRANCH=master
